            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="1" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="1" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>



&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;



Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through the hole in your drywall in the living room, and underneath your kitchen sink and dishwasher.  



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $180&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.9GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

&lt;b&gt;Wall Insulation:&lt;/b&gt;



The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 3.5 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid XPS insulation to the outside of your home to bring the total insulation value to R27.



I recommend doing this upgrade when you need to replace your siding. This would also be a good time to replace your windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of rigid XPS insulation to your walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $700&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;23.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



Adding less insulation may also be more cost-effective, but I recommend that you add at least R9 to your walls. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

&lt;b&gt;Ceilings (Gable):&lt;/b&gt;



The attic of your house was assumed to have 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding an additional R30 of insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding an additional R30 to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $280&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$375 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

&lt;b&gt;Crawlspace:&lt;/b&gt;



I have assumed that the floor above your crawl space is 2x10 joists with R20 insulation and 2 inches of rigid XPS insulation, and your crawlspace walls are 2x4 framing with no insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R14) fiberglass batt insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls, and adding 4 inches (R14) fiberglass batt insulation between the joists for a total of R34, plus the 2 inches of XTPS that is below the joists. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing the R20 batt insulation that is likely between the joists of the floor above your crawlspace with10 inches (R32) fiberglass batt insulation and adding R14 batt insulation to the walls of your crawlspace, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $85&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. This is less than the additional R23 required to qualify for rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for the floor above your crawl space, but you could be eligible for &lt;b&gt;$500&lt;/b&gt; for insulating your crawlspace walls. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the floor and increase its insulation value by a minimum of RSI 4.2 (R-24). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate. To qualify for the rebate for insulating the crawlspace walls, you must insulate 100 percent of the walls and add a minimum of R10 insulation. 





For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;





</foundation>
                <windows>

&lt;b&gt;Windows:&lt;/b&gt;



As all of your windows are older double pane sliding windows, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



As your windows appear to be in good physical shape, I would not recommend replacing them at this time. Window upgrades are often done at the same time as a siding and insulation upgrade.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all 10 windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $120&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

</windows>
                <doors />
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Propane Furnace:&lt;/b&gt;



Your current propane fired furnace was originally rated at 80% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $650&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;22.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

&lt;b&gt;Solar Upgrade:&lt;/b&gt;



Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $890&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;10.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt; GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system. You would be eligible for 1/3 of the purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year. Applicants are strongly encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;



</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Wood Stove:&lt;/b&gt;



When determining the rating of your house, only electricity and fossil fuels are considered. However, recognizing that your wood stove represents a significant portion of your heating load, I have calculated the savings for the use of your wood stove for 50% of your heating load.



For your house as currently rated prior to undertaking any upgrades, and using your wood stove to meet 50% of the heating load, I calculated a savings in energy costs of approximately $1400 per year. 



If you were to do all of the renovations suggested in the renovation upgrade report, and continue using your wood stove to meet 50% of the heating load, I calculated a savings in energy costs of approximately $345 per year.  



All savings are based on our standard fuel prices. Depending on how you get cord wood, you may find your savings are different from those calculated here. </AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
